  "unit_key": "g3-sec1-g5",
  "position": 4,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Conversion between square and cubic metric units",
  "summary": "Apply squared and cubed conversion factors to areas and volumes.",
  "outcomes": [
    "5.4"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g5-lesson-03"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Distinguish linear, square and cubic conversion factors.",
    "Convert reliably between cm² and m².",
    "Convert reliably between cm³ and m³."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g5-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-54-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Apply squared and cubed conversion factors to areas and volumes. Keep every measurement in compatible units before substituting."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "1\\text{ m}^2=10\\,000\\text{ cm}^2,\\qquad1\\text{ m}^3=1\\,000\\,000\\text{ cm}^3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-04-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows a cube with edges of 1 m, written as 100 cm. For lengths, apply the factor once: 1 m = 100 cm. For areas, apply it twice (square it): one face is 100 cm by 100 cm, so 1 m² = 100² = 10 000 cm². For volumes, apply it three times (cube it): the cube holds 100 × 100 × 100 = 1 000 000 cm³, so 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-04-diagram"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The same idea works for millimetres. Since 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 cm² = 10² = 100 mm² and 1 cm³ = 10³ = 1 000 mm³. For capacity, 1 litre = 1 000 cm³ and 1 millilitre = 1 cm³."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-04-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Write the conversion as a power before multiplying. Moving to a smaller unit increases the numerical value; moving to a larger unit decreases it. Convert all dimensions before using an area or volume formula, rather than converting the final result inconsistently."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-54-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: square metres to square centimetres",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert 2.5 m² to cm²."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Square the length factor: 1 m² = 100² cm² = 10 000 cm²."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2.5\\times10\\,000=25\\,000"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "2.5\\text{ m}^2=25\\,000\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Changing to a smaller unit gives a larger number. Using the length factor 100 alone would give 250, which is 100 times too small."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-04-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: cubic metres to litres",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert 0.024 m³ to cm³ and then to litres."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cube the length factor: 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "0.024\\times1\\,000\\,000=24\\,000\\text{ cm}^3"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Since 1 litre = 1 000 cm³, divide by 1 000."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "24\\,000\\div1\\,000=24\\text{ litres}"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "0.024 m³ is 24 dm³, and each dm³ (a 10 cm cube) is one litre."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-04-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not use the linear factor 100 for area or volume. Attach the exponent to the conversion factor. A quick direction check catches many errors: the number of square centimetres in one square metre must be much greater than one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-54-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert 0.4 m³ to cm³."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "400000",
        "canonical_latex": "400000",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Multiply by 1 000 000: 0.4 m³ = 400 000 cm³."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-54-recall-mm",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding: millimetres",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Convert 350 mm² to cm²."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "3.5",
        "canonical_latex": "3.5",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1 cm² = 10² mm² = 100 mm², so divide by 100: 350 mm² = 3.5 cm²."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-04-summary",
      "position": 9,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Length: × 100 for m to cm. Area: × 100² = 10 000. Volume: × 100³ = 1 000 000. For cm to mm, use 10, 10² = 100 and 10³ = 1 000."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Multiply when changing to a smaller unit and divide when changing to a larger one, then check that the direction makes sense. 1 litre = 1 000 cm³."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
